<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<UserControl
    x:Class="MeetingTranscriber.App.ChannelStrip"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:local="using:MeetingTranscriber.App"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!-- One channel: the picker that chooses its source, and under it the meter of what that source
         is bringing in. `docs/design.md` §Where it goes is the whole reason the two are one control
         — **the meter is pinned to the control that chooses its source**, so somebody picks, looks,
         and picks the next one. That is the case of the three processes called Teams, and the two
         drawn apart turn it into a memory test.

         One control used twice, because two meters is the contract: channel 0 is the others and
         channel 1 is you, and there are two of them for ever. It knows it is a channel and never
         which — the role, what the picker offers and what the picker is called all come
         from the window, which is what keeps one control from having to know which language it is
         being read in.

         No literal and no colour, size or corner of its own: every text is set by the window, and
         every brush and rank names an Olivo key. The four numbers here are spacings and widths,
         which the system does not name and no screen is held to; the one number that is this
         control's own anatomy — how much of itself the card keeps once its source is gone — is in
         ChannelStrip.xaml.cs beside the line that applies it, for the reason ChannelMeter's five
         are beside theirs.

         What the channel is **actually** capturing is not restated under the bar. While nothing has
         moved it is what the picker says, and the moment it stops being that, the line the window
         writes beside this one says so in pico, with both names in it. A quiet grey repeat of the
         same fact under every meter would be the screen saying the same thing twice and saying it
         loudest where nothing had happened.

         The artboard puts a fourth thing on this row — *se escucha*, right-aligned in olivo — and
         it is not drawn: the design allows the level in mono instead, so the word is not needed to
         say the channel is alive. *Sin señal* stands where the level does, under the bar, like *se
         cortó a las 08:12* — a source that died says that where its level was, because that is the
         place on the component the level vacated. The pill still stops at its own content. -->

    <Grid RowSpacing="10">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <Grid Grid.Row="0" ColumnSpacing="9">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <!-- What this channel is: the others, or you. It is named by what it hears and never by
                 the number Deepgram reports it back as: the channel index is the provider's and
                 belongs to the files, and a person choosing a source needs the role. -->
            <TextBlock
                x:Name="Role"
                Grid.Column="0"
                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                Style="{StaticResource BodyText}"
                FontWeight="SemiBold" />

            <!-- The pill. It carries no header, which is what lets it stand at the control rank's
                 34 — what it chooses is the text to its left. A screen reader has no label to
                 read off those, so the window gives it one by name. -->
            <!-- Left and not stretched, with a floor and no ceiling. The artboard runs the pill
                 from the role to just short of a status word; there is none on this row (*sin
                 señal* is under the bar, where the level is), so the pill stops at its own
                 content. No ceiling
                 because a `ContentPresenter` cannot trim: a maximum width is a device name cut
                 flat at the pill's edge, reading as a name that is still going. -->
            <local:DropDown
                x:Name="Picker"
                Grid.Column="1"
                MinWidth="260"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                Style="{StaticResource DropDown}"
                DropDownOpened="OnOpened"
                SelectionChanged="OnChosen" />
        </Grid>

        <local:ChannelMeter x:Name="Meter" Grid.Row="1" />
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
